<p>My friends, you might have noticed I did not post anything last week. <a href="https://pkm.social/@ellane/116979180706979011" target="_blank">It’s been a rough and rocky time</a>. The miracle we were praying for did not come to pass (again), but miracles have surrounded us nonetheless. Feeling truly grateful for the kindness of people who know us and those who don’t. Family, community, God: we are being cared for, comforted, carried.</p>
<p>This week’s thoughts are on the theme of living a slow and intentional life.</p>
<h3 id="forster-lists-are-continuing-to-reduce-my-mental-load">Forster lists are continuing to reduce my mental load</h3>
<p>I’m slightly less of a Forster-newbie than <a href="https://ellanew.com/2026/07/20/ptpl-217-forster-lists-keep-me-organised-screen-free" class="wikilink">2 weeks ago</a>, and growing in appreciation for the method with each day that passes. No more separate work and home lists, everything is combined. Rewriting tasks is something I look forward to, not something I wish I didn’t have to do.</p>
<p>The simplicity of this system for choosing tasks to do right now is quietly stunning.</p>
<h3 id="the-social-etiquette-of-making-notes">The social etiquette of making notes</h3>
<p>Here’s a thought on social etiquette for note makers, for people with the ability to choose between analog and digital tools —</p>
<p><em>Writing in a real book is much more discreet in certain situations</em>. Think funerals, church, or around children or elderly relatives.</p>
<p>Not caring what other people think can be a noble thing, but so is choosing paper over digital when the occasion calls for it.<br>
Keep in mind that, no matter your reason for doing so, pulling out a device has the potential to distract those unfamiliar with it or detract from solemn occasions.</p>
<p>Having spent a lot of time with my easily distractible and largely screen-free grandchildren (ages 0 — 9) lately, I can tell you this means even more to me now than it did when I originally penned the above thought.</p>
<p>My current notebook of choice is an <span class="small-caps">A6</span> Hobonichi grid paired with a fine nib fountain pen and a compact mechanical pencil. Also a 15cm ruler, and a Tombow fine point eraser pen. All of this fits nicely into my Orbitkey cross body sling bag. I have the olive colour.</p>
